            id: 'sc-1-1',
            title: 'The Center of Limbo',
            content: `Limbo had no center. Every Primal who had ever tried to walk from one end of it to the other had found themselves walking back from where they started, or walking into somewhere they had already been, or somewhere that had not existed until they arrived. The philosophers of later ages—those who had access to the concept of philosophy—would describe this as the void dimension being folded inside itself. The Primals did not describe it at all. They simply existed in it, the way water does not describe a lake.

Three Primordials occupied whatever Limbo considered its center. They did not live there by choice. They had no relationship to choice. They existed there because they were there, and they were there because they were the principles that kept Limbo's structure coherent.

Kazemi was a dragon in the oldest sense—not a creature with wings and territorial instincts, but the thing that is larger than the sky it flies through. His body was the first material: not a material, but the material. The root substance from which every tool, every structure, every shaped thing in Limbo was ultimately derived. Where Kazemi moved, making became possible. Where he was still, the fabric held.

Gaia was the cycling force. Not merely vitality—the principle by which things that had been one thing became another, and that transformation was not loss but change. Life force was her nature. So was death force. She was the reason both existed in sequence and not simultaneously. She was not gentle. She was precise.

Cronus was the weight of what had already happened. In his presence, the past was real. Events that had occurred could not be undone, rewritten, or erased. Causality ran cleanly. The chain of consequence held together. Without him, the structure of time in Limbo would have been negotiable—the way dreams are negotiable—and a universe of negotiable time cannot produce anything permanent.

None of the three had named themselves or been named by others. The Primals who existed around them had words for the forces—the Material, the Cycle, the Weight—but these were descriptions, not identifiers. A Primal referring to Kazemi did not say "Kazemi." A Primal simply gestured toward the largest thing in any space and understood.

This was the world before the child.`,
