            content: `He arrived the way lightning arrives—between one moment and the next, with no visible preparation and no observable origin. A child of approximately twelve, sitting in the space where there had been no child a moment before.

He was not a Primal. That was the first wrong thing. He had the composition of something that had come from outside—from the living world beyond Limbo's boundary. His body was warm in the wrong way. Made of a kind of matter that had not been in the void before.

The Primals nearest to where he appeared gathered at a distance and watched him with the patient, geological attention of beings who have no urgency about most things.

He looked back at them and said something. They did not understand the language. The sounds were elaborately structured—subject and verb, fine distinctions of meaning built over millennia of Orian linguistic development—and to the Primals, who communicated in older ways, it was simply sound.

Then he said something else.

Something short. The kind of thing a frightened child says when surrounded by creatures he does not understand and is trying to explain that he means no harm. He said it with complete sincerity, the way a child does when they have not yet learned that the world does not take sincerity into account.

And reality responded.

Not to the words. To the nature behind them. He was not a caster of spells in the sense that would be developed later, in any structured sense. He had shaped nothing. He had simply expressed something true, with complete conviction, using the only language he had. And because he was what he was—a Conceptual of Magic, though no one had any framework yet to know what that meant—his expression was not an expression. It was a description of how things were. Things became how he described them.

The three Primordials ceased.

Not gradually. Not with struggle. Between one moment and the next, the center of Limbo was without Kazemi, without Gaia, without Cronus.

The child sat in the silence they had left and understood, through the way the Primals around him reacted—the patient attention becoming something harder and pointed—that he had done something he could not take back.

He ran.`,
